WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.610
- So Representative Eric Swalwell

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:04.390
has filed suit against former
President Donald Trump,

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:06.530
for basically inciting a riot.

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:08.460
He has filed a civil suit
to try and get damages

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.760
for all of the things that he claims

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.050
that President Trump did
as a result of January 6th.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:18.000
Now, this lawsuit is not on great footing

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.830
and is probably going to get dismissed

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:20.940
for several different reasons.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.130
First, presidents have absolute immunity

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.020
from all civil actions taken

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.030
as a result of official
acts while president.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.940
The case law is pretty
much settled on this point,

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.380
you cannot civilly sue a former president

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:33.380
for actions that they took

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.270
that were part of their official duties.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.040
It appears that a
prosecutor could go after

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.230
a former president for
criminal actions taken

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:40.520
but not a civil suit.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.353
And additionally,

00:00:41.353 --> 00:00:42.880
this lawsuit would have
to get over the hurdle

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.093
to prove that President
Trump committed incitement

00:00:45.093 --> 00:00:47.920
that his words were not
protected by the First Amendment.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.340
And that is likely to
be dispositive as well.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.960
So this lawsuit is probably
not going anywhere.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.080
So if you're Eric Swalwell,
I won't see you in court,

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.680
but if you don't subscribe,
I will see you in court.

